A||N||3||Two88GTs||88 V6 EGR tube replacement alternative||||1||
Z||000000||Two88GTs||07-29-2001||01:00 AM||||I had been trying to find out why my cold idle was 1600-1700 rpm on my black 88GT while my red GT's cold idle was 1400-1500.  After ruling out the vacuum lines I looked underneath the upper intake and behold, a half inch crack on the EGR tube.  I was going to remove it and have it welded at a shop(but that would have cost me some $ plus I was in a tinkering mood so I grabbed an EGR tube from a 3rd Gen 2.8L Firebird/Camaro from a recent trip to the junkyard.  I had been told that it was a suitable replacement provided that you elongate the bolt holes on the EGR end of the Camaro/Firebird tube.  An hour and a couple of dremel bits later, it fit!<BR>Although it doesn't have the corrugated heat shield that the Fiero EGR tube has, the Camaro/Firbird tube looks MUCH more robust and may be less prone to cracking.  Best of all, they're plentiful at the junkyards.  Now both cars idle under 1500 rpm.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||07-29-2001||11:41 AM||||Was it long enough to fit without a "stretch"? I put an earlier Fiero EGR tube on my 88 Formula, but it really wasn't "happy". I expect it will probably crack at some point. I've also heard about the Camaro/Firebird EGR tubes but figured that they would probably still be 1/2" "short" on an 88.<BR>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Two88GTs||07-30-2001||01:12 AM||||The Camaro/Firebird tubes are definitely long enough but you do have to add a bend here or there to fit.  Thankfully, I had the broken EGR tube as a guide and I got the new tube to fit the first time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||07-30-2001||09:20 AM||||Good tip. Thanks! ||||reg||14
